<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ninlil</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ninlil was a Sumerian goddess, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> and mother of the moon god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a>. Worshipped beside Enlil at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, she was identified with Sud, the patron goddess of Shuruppak. A Sumerian myth tells how Enlil was banished to the underworld for seizing her and how she followed him there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> later identified her with the wife of Ashur.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ninlil was a Mesopotamian goddess regarded as the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>, and the meaning of her name is uncertain. A late explanatory text translates it as &quot;lady of the breeze&quot;, which matches a theory that Enlil's name means &quot;lord wind&quot;. At an early date she was identified with the goddess Sud of Shuruppak, who was also associated with Enlil, and eventually fully absorbed her. In the myth Enlil and Sud, Ninlil is the name Sud received after marrying Enlil, and Nisaba, the goddess of writing, and her husband Haya are her parents. Her Akkadian name was Mullilu, in Neo-Assyrian sources Mullissu, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> transcribed as Mylitta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>'s wife Ninlil shared many of his functions, above all the declaring of destinies, and stood at the top of the pantheon. A late hymn calls her ruler of earth and heaven and says that Enlil made no decision without her. Kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> considered both of them the source of royal authority. She was the mother of Nergal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a> and Ninurta. She was chiefly worshiped at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> and nearby Tummal, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekur">Ekur</a> temple complex held her Kiur and the Eitimaku, described as her bedchamber.</p><p>In the myth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> and Ninlil, the inexperienced Ninlil is warned by her mother Nunbaršegunu to avoid Enlil. He seduces and impregnates her, and the fifty great gods and the seven gods of destinies judge him ritually impure and exile him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>. Ninlil follows him and gives birth to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a>, Nergal, Ninazu and Enbilulu. Scholars debate whether his crime was rape or premarital sex.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Ninlil was taken into Hurrian religion, where she and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> were among the primeval gods and could be invoked as witnesses of treaties. In Syrian cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, Emar and Ugarit she was closely associated with Shalash, the spouse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a>, a god regarded as analogous to Enlil. From the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-i">Tiglath-Pileser I</a> onward she was viewed as the wife of the Assyrian head god Ashur, and in this role developed into Mullissu, who was conflated with Ishtar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, also recast as Ashur's consort.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ninlil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Langdon took Nintud, the chief goddess of Kesh, for a form of <b>Ninlil</b>: the Ninlil of Kesh, where her character as goddess of begetting was emphasised, the goddess bearing different names in different places.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kesh-temple-hymn">Kesh Temple Hymn</a></li><li><blockquote>It ends in praise of Enlil and his consort <b>Ninlil</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a></li><li><blockquote>The city was dedicated to Sud, later identified with <b>Ninlil</b>, the wife of Enlil.</blockquote><a href="/state/shuruppak">Shuruppak</a></li></ul></section>
